<article class="env-definition" id="0030">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0030">Definition <span data-tag="0030">4.21.2</span>.</a> Let $(I, \leq )$ be a preordered set. Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A <em>system over $I$ in $\mathcal{C}$</em>, sometimes called a <em>inductive system over $I$ in $\mathcal{C}$</em> is given by objects $M_ i$ of $\mathcal{C}$ and for every $i \leq i'$ a morphism $f_{ii'} : M_ i \to M_{i'}$ such that $f_{ii} = \text{id}$ and such that $f_{ii''} = f_{i'i''} \circ f_{i i'}$ whenever $i \leq i' \leq i''$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
An <em>inverse system over $I$ in $\mathcal{C}$</em>, sometimes called a <em>projective system over $I$ in $\mathcal{C}$</em> is given by objects $M_ i$ of $\mathcal{C}$ and for every $i' \leq i$ a morphism $f_{ii'} : M_ i \to M_{i'}$ such that $f_{ii} = \text{id}$ and such that $f_{ii''} = f_{i'i''} \circ f_{i i'}$ whenever $i'' \leq i' \leq i$. (Note reversal of inequalities.) 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> We will say $(M_ i, f_{ii'})$ is a (inverse) system over $I$ to denote this. The maps $f_{ii'}$ are sometimes called the <em>transition maps</em>. </p>
</article>